<p>
Bozhidar Batsov has announced the <a href="https://emacsredux.com/blog/2026/11/25/emacs-prelude-2-2/">release of Prelude 2.2</a>. For those of you who don’t know, Prelude is a prepackaged configuration that is simpler and more flexible than configurations like Doom and Spacemacs. It’s not, I suppose, as full featured as Doom and Spacemacs but it’s less opinionated and easier to customize.
</p>
<p>
In general, I’m not a fan of prepackaged configurations but for someone just starting out, something like Prelude may make sense. In any event, Prelude has been around for 15 years and is a mature product. A product, furthermore, developed and maintained by an Emacs luminary.
</p>
<p>
One of the most obvious changes in this version is the change to the <a href="https://github.com/bbatsov/tokyo-night-emacs">Tokyo Night theme</a>. It replaces the traditional Zenburn theme. I hate both of them—especially Tokyo Night—but plenty of people disagree. In any event, it’s easy to change the theme so as usual with Emacs, you can have it your way.
</p>
<p>
One of the most significant changes is that Batsov has tried hard to make Prelude more familiar to “vanilla” Emacs users. One simple example of that is his use of use-package= instead of the Prelude specific <code>prelude-require-package</code> that previous versions used.
</p>
<p>
Another nice feature is <code>prelude-doctor</code> that troubleshoots your configuration and finds missing prerequisites. That makes getting Prelude setup easier.
</p>
<p>
Batsov has also sped up the startup process although, like me, he continues to insist startup time doesn’t really matter. There are several other changes so take a look at Batsov’s post for the details.</p>
